{"findings": [{"title": "CRITICAL: Data Leakage — Target Labels Embedded Directly in Training Text", "body": "Cell 38 appends target labels to training text: `train['text_processed'] = train['text_processed'] + \" [target_\" + train['target'].astype(str) + \"]\"`. The tokenizer (Cell 40) feeds this augmented text to the model. The model achieves near-perfect training accuracy by reading the `[target_X]` token instead of learning from tweet content. This contaminates training metrics AND validation metrics (validation comes from the same processed training set), rendering model selection meaningless. Final test predictions fail because the learned `[target_X]` tokens don't exist in real test data.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "CRITICAL: Stochastic Evaluation — model.train() Called During Prediction/Validation", "body": "Cell 40, `StochasticEvalTrainer.prediction_step()` calls `model.train()` (line 75) before `super().prediction_step()`. This keeps dropout and batch normalization layers active during all evaluation and prediction. Validation metrics used for early stopping and `load_best_model_at_end` are non-deterministic and unreliable. `model.eval()` is never called anywhere in the entire codebase. Test predictions are also stochastic.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "CRITICAL: Stochastic Data Augmentation Applied to Validation and Test Sets", "body": "Cell 40 `tokenize()` function uses `np.random.rand()` for augmentation (35% word dropping, 20% appending 'storm') and applies it to BOTH `train_dataset` and `test_dataset` via `.map(tokenize, batched=True)`. Since validation data is created by splitting `train_dataset` (which already received augmentation), the validation set also receives stochastic augmentation. Test predictions are doubly non-deterministic: augmented input + model in training mode.", "confidence": 1.0}, {"title": "Augmentation Applied Before Train/Validation Split Contaminates Validation", "body": "Cell 40 line 33: `train_dataset.map(tokenize, 